@@ Section 4.2.2
-The Calendar arm is informative for interpretation. A pure ``mass near the margin'' explanation would predict that any additive private item should have a larger effect in Far communities if more adults are close to the participation cutoff there. The Calendar effect does not vary with distance, while the distance interaction appears only for public signals. This pattern points to a mechanism tied to observability and social image rather than to a generic density at the margin effect.
+The Calendar arm is informative for interpretation. A pure ``mass near the margin'' explanation would predict that any additive private item should have a larger effect in Far communities if more adults are close to the participation cutoff there. The Calendar effect does not vary with distance, while the distance interaction appears only for public signals. This pattern also helps address the baseline imbalance in past deworming reported in Appendix Table~\ref{tab:baseline-bal}: Calendar communities display a similar Far--Close pattern in prior deworming to the public-signal arms, but Calendar take-up is flat across distance. Moreover, although the baseline and take-up samples cannot be linked at the individual level, cluster-level baseline prior-deworming rates can be merged to the full take-up sample. Controlling for the cluster shares ever dewormed and dewormed in the past year leaves the treatment-by-distance pattern intact: for monitored take-up, the Bracelet and Ink Far--Close interactions are 11.8 and 10.5 percentage points, while the Calendar interaction is 2.7 percentage points. Using endline self-reported deworming as an alternative outcome produces the same qualitative pattern (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:prior-deworming-robustness}). This points to a mechanism tied to observability and social image rather than to a generic density at the margin effect or differential prior deworming propensity.

